BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Creative Circuits
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260702T190000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260702T220000
UID:blogto-236781@blogto.com
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://blogto-production2-baselayer-display.blo
 gto.com/events/2026/06/20/original_image199c735b-8559-42fe-a2d6-2345fa6cbf
 cc.jpg
DESCRIPTION:Toronto's creative community event with industry speakers\, ne
 tworking\, and art vibes!\nAn evening for creatives and the curious to min
 gle.\nWhether you're crafting interactive experiences\, curious how creato
 rs are merging art and technology\, or simply interested in the future of 
 human-machine collaboration\, this event is for you.\nSCHEDULE\n7:00 - Doo
 rs open\nGrab a drink\, settle in\, and watch select renders from "Decompo
 sitions for Computers"\, an artistic exploration by transmedia artist and 
 creative technologist Ali Phi\, which utilizes complex data sets to expose
  the unseen consequences of our digital footprints.\n7:30 - Decompositions
  for Computers (Ali Phi)\nAli Phi's practice spans digital installations\,
  audiovisual performances\, and generative art. Drawing on programming\, d
 ata visualization\, and interactive media\, he explores the interplay betw
 een geometry\, light\, science\, and cultural memory\, reimagining ancient
  motifs and Middle Eastern rituals through a futuristic lens. In this talk
 \, Ali discusses his research-driven approach\, and shares recent works pr
 esented at international contemporary art venues.\n8:00 - Photosynthetic C
 yborgs (Vladimir Kanic)\nWhat if algae could speak? Vladimir Kanic present
 s living sculptures that capture carbon and explores how AI is being used 
 to translate the biochemical signals of algae into a new interspecies lang
 uage.\n8:30 - Antique Intelligence: Interactive Fiction in Tangible Spaces
  (Kyle Duffield)\nKyle Duffield is an interactive experience designer who 
 creates playful\, socially driven immersive installations. In this talk\, 
 he explores how embedding AI agents in reanimated vintage technologies can
  innovate interactive storytelling\, while confronting pressing humanitari
 an crises — such as the distraction economy\, and the imminent invasion 
 of interdimensional shapeshifters.\n9:00 - Mingling\, networking\nMeet coo
 l people and vibe.\n- $17 Pre-Sale (until 7 days before the event - Limite
 d quantities)\n- $20 General Admission (Limited quantities)\n- $25 General
  Admission (Day of event @ door)\nABOUT US\nBloom Immersive is a Toronto-b
 ased creative technology company merging spatial computing and gen AI to d
 esign intelligent environments that turn audiences from passive viewers in
 to active participants.\nCreative Circuits is our creative technology spea
 ker series and networking event where industry leading start-ups\, as well
  as Toronto's biggest digital creators\, come to present their ideas and s
 howcase what they're working on.\nFollow us on Instagram @bloom_immersive 
 or TikTok @bloom_immersive\nE mail: info@bloomimmersive.com
LOCATION:ZUI Beer Bar on Bloor
URL:https://www.blogto.com/events/creative-circuits-toronto-5/
X-SOURCE:blogTO
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Evil persists\, but I persist more - La Pupila's July Artist of th
 e Month Exhibition at Loohoo
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260702T120000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260702T180000
UID:blogto-237100@blogto.com
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://blogto-production2-baselayer-display.blo
 gto.com/events/2026/06/26/original_imagea9922cb0-e881-4d32-ad6f-0ee477d3da
 c4.jpg
DESCRIPTION:This July\, Loohoo is proud to present Evil persists\, but I p
 ersist more\, a solo exhibition by La Pupila\, a Colombian visual artist b
 ased in Tkaronto/Toronto.\nJoin us for the opening reception on Thursday J
 uly 9 from 6 to 8:30PM to meet the artist\, see the work in person\, and s
 top by the Artist Shrine for stickers\, temp tattoos\, coin purses\, and m
 ore.\nWorking across painting\, murals\, tattooing\, and printmaking\, La 
 Pupila's work explores emotions and memories through symbols\, distorted f
 orms\, and evocative faces. Her visual language is poetic and tender\, cre
 ating space for introspection and connection.\nThis collection was shaped 
 by feelings of melancholy and anger\, and also by hope. Inspired by the po
 wer and beauty of nature\, the works evoke the feeling of being part of th
 e mountain\, the flames that pass through it\, the water and the collectiv
 e effort to calm the fire. And of finding\, after every catastrophe\, a wi
 nd of hope.\nLa Pupila's work is already woven into Toronto's public space
 s. Some highlights include a mural celebrating women in skateboarding with
  the Sisters of Shred\, a commission for YYZ Gallery exploring Venus as a 
 utopia for women and non-binary people\, and collaborative murals in Littl
 e Portugal and on Barton Street reflecting on community\, care\, and colle
 ctive strength. You can see more of her work at lapupila.ca.\nThe exhibiti
 on runs July 2 to August 2 at Loohoo\, 1380 Queen St W in Parkdale.\nFree 
 to attend. RSVP appreciated\, follow the Eventbrite link\nLoohoo is open T
 hursday to Saturday 12 to 6PM and Sunday 12 to 5PM.\nFollow us on Instagra
 m for updates @shop.loohoo @lapupila999
LOCATION:Loohoo
URL:https://www.blogto.com/events/evil-persists-but-i-persist-more-la-pupi
 las-july-artist-of-the-month-exhibition-at-loohoo-toronto/
X-SOURCE:blogTO
END:VEVENT
