BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Taste of Little Jamaica Launches This Summer: Two Beloved Caribbea
 n Food Festivals Unite to Revitalize Little Jamaica
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260718T120000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260719T000000
UID:blogto-234860@blogto.com
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://blogto-production2-baselayer-display.blo
 gto.com/events/2026/05/10/original_image3ff0376d-aea6-4e94-8500-8d21a49aa0
 f9.jpg
DESCRIPTION:Toronto Patty Fest and Taste The Caribbean Return Together for
  One Massive Cultural Food Celebration on Eglinton West\nLittle Jamaica is
  cooking again.\nAfter years of construction\, economic hardship\, broken 
 promises\, and cultural displacement\, two of Little Jamaica’s most belo
 ved grassroots festivals are joining forces to bring life\, flavour\, musi
 c\, and people back to the neighbourhood with the launch of the very first
  Taste of Little Jamaica\, happening Saturday\, July 18 from 12PM to 12AM 
 along Eglinton Avenue West between Oakwood Avenue and Glenholme Avenue.\nT
 his new one-day festival marks the long-awaited return of Taste The Caribb
 ean Food Festival\, absent for two years\, and Toronto Patty Fest\, return
 ing after one year away. Both festivals previously operated independently 
 in Little Jamaica for two successful editions each\, drawing thousands of 
 attendees to celebrate Caribbean food and culture in the heart of the city
 .\nNow\, for the first time ever\, the festivals have merged their resourc
 es\, audiences\, and community power to create one large-scale cultural fo
 od experience rooted in survival\, collaboration\, and love for the commun
 ity.\n“This is bigger than a food festival\,” say organizers. “This 
 is about economic survival for Little Jamaica. We were told the LRT would 
 save the neighbourhood\, but many businesses are still struggling. The rea
 lity is people are moving underground and bypassing the community entirely
 . Little Jamaica has not bounced back.”\nOrganizers stress that the fest
 ival has been built with little to no institutional support and without Ci
 ty funding.\n“There is no money waiting for us. No big safety net. These
  festivals continue because we believe in our community and because we kno
 w how important these events are for local businesses\, entrepreneurs\, an
 d cultural visibility. Alone\, neither festival could continue at the scal
 e the community deserves. Together\, we stand a fighting chance.”\nTaste
  of Little Jamaica will transform the avenue into a lively celebration of 
 Caribbean culture featuring:\nThe Toronto Patty Pavilion — celebrating t
 he legendary Caribbean patty with vendors\, and creative patty experiences
 \nThe Taste The Caribbean Food Village — showcasing authentic and modern
  Caribbean cuisine from across the diaspora\nThe Afro-Caribbean Cultural M
 arketplace — highlighting local artisans\, makers\, and community entrep
 reneurs\nLive music\, DJs\, family games\, cultural activities\, performan
 ces\, and programming for all ages from afternoon into midnight\nThe festi
 val is expected to draw thousands from across Toronto and beyond\, bringin
 g much-needed foot traffic and economic activity directly into the Little 
 Jamaica corridor.\nOrganizers are also actively seeking sponsors\, partner
 s\, and supporters who believe in cultural communities\, grassroots econom
 ic development\, and the preservation of Little Jamaica as one of Toronto
 ’s most important Caribbean cultural districts.\n“We are doing this fo
 r the love of the culture and the future of the neighbourhood. But culture
  alone cannot carry the financial burden forever. We need sponsors and sup
 porters who understand that investing in Little Jamaica means investing in
  Toronto itself.”\nVendor applications are open!\nFor sponsorship opport
 unities\, please email: afrocaribbeanfm@gmail.com\nEVENT DETAILS\nEvent: T
 aste of Little Jamaica\nDate: Saturday\, July 18\, 2026\nTime: 12PM – 12
 AM\nLocation: Eglinton Avenue West between Oakwood Ave & Glenholme Ave\, T
 oronto\nFor media inquires contact: Lori Beazer\, afrocaribbeanfm@gmail.co
 m (416) 454-3848
URL:https://www.blogto.com/events/taste-of-little-jamaica-launches-this-su
 mmer-two-beloved-caribbean-food-festivals-unite-to-revitalize-little-jamai
 ca-toronto/
X-SOURCE:blogTO
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:The Decline of Western Civilization - 45th Anniversary
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260718T190000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260718T210000
UID:blogto-236607@blogto.com
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://blogto-production2-baselayer-display.blo
 gto.com/events/2026/06/16/original_image0b5b8091-4160-4b27-ac91-3315c69023
 31.jpg
DESCRIPTION:"A bracing\, stimulating and technically superb close-up look 
 at the L.A. punk scene." – Variety\nTheir message is brutally clear: Des
 troy the old and make way for the new. This is the punk's violent revoluti
 on\; their lawless world. This is The Decline of Western Civilization\, a 
 riveting\, unflinching account of the punk rock phenomenon and its alienat
 ed\, reactionary subculture. Penelope Spheeris' fierce\, bleak portrait do
 cuments L.A.'s infamous punk bands as they perform on stage and discuss th
 eir lives\, music and philosophy off stage.\nThrough interviews with punk 
 fans\, music critics and club owners\, it is a crucial\, compelling statem
 ent of one of the most significant and influential youth movements and mus
 ical transformations. It is perhaps a prophetic glimpse of the forces that
  will inherit our world…. Witness THE DECLINE.
LOCATION:Hot Docs Ted Rogers Cinema
URL:https://www.blogto.com/events/the-decline-of-western-civilization-45th
 -anniversary-toronto/
X-SOURCE:blogTO
END:VEVENT
