BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Jane Austen's Northanger Abbey
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260806T193000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260806T210000
UID:blogto-236598@blogto.com
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://blogto-production2-baselayer-display.blo
 gto.com/events/2026/06/16/original_imaged8a69feb-decf-4514-a847-24a2a253e8
 be.jpg
DESCRIPTION:Northanger Abbey\, Jane Austen’s satire of the Gothic novel 
 craze\, is brought to life in a hilarious\, whirlwind production by the di
 rector of last year's hit The 39 Steps!\nCatherine Morland loves reading t
 he latest books but she lets her imagination run wild\, finding Gothic mys
 tery in the most mundane corners of England. As the clichés of the Horrid
  Novels spill into her reality\, Catherine's simple holiday is transformed
  into a personal adventure filled with mischief and romance.\nPresented ou
 tdoors at the iconic Greek Theatre in Guild Park & Gardens\, this new adap
 tation of Austen's charming coming-of-age story is a funny and fast-paced 
 journey of first love and self-discovery.\nGuild Festival Theatre’s 2026
  Season is presented by ARC Insurance & Financial Services Inc.\nAdapted &
  Directed by Tyler J. Seguin\nCostume Design by Alex Amini\nProduction Des
 ign by Elisia Evans\nLighting Design by Honey Hoseiny\nSound Design by Sha
 wn Piccione\nStage Manager Kiera Doerksen Smith\nChoreographer & Assistant
  Stage Manager Leah Wilton\nAssistant Director Bonnie Duff\nFeaturing\nGeo
 rgia Findlay\nIsaiah Kolundzic\nAndrew Prashad\nKiana Woo\nAugust 6th-23rd
 \, $16.25-$32.50
LOCATION:Guild Festival Theatre
URL:https://www.blogto.com/events/jane-austens-northanger-abbey-toronto/
X-SOURCE:blogTO
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Emerging Artist Spotlight
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260807T193000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260807T220000
UID:blogto-237011@blogto.com
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://blogto-production2-baselayer-display.blo
 gto.com/events/2026/06/25/original_imagea9185463-cadc-4b07-af74-56b17ee218
 7c.jpg
DESCRIPTION:The 25th Annual Putnam County Spelling Bee is culminating perf
 ormance of the teen music theatre program.\nA Tony Award-winning musical c
 omedy following six quirky\, endearing middle-schoolers competing in a reg
 ional spelling bee — each desperately hoping to win. Witty\, fast-paced\
 , and surprisingly tender\, it's an interactive\, laugh-filled experience 
 where the audience becomes part of the fun. A crowd-pleaser for all ages.\
 nAugust 7th-8th\, 7:30 PM\nSPSS · 525 Bloor St E\, Toronto\nTickets $10
 –$40\nThe Marriage of Figaro is the culminating performance of the Emerg
 ing artist (18+) Opera Intensive Program. The Marriage of Figaro is one of
  opera’s most beloved comedies — a Mozart masterpiece based on Beaumar
 chais’ scandalous play. First performed in Vienna in 1786\, the opera ha
 s captivated audiences for nearly two and a half centuries.\nRich with ico
 nic arias and ensemble pieces\, The Marriage of Figaro\, sung in Italian w
 ith English recitatives and surtitles\, is celebrated for its emotional de
 pth\, brilliant comic timing\, and sheer musical joy. The score features s
 ome of Mozart’s most inspired writing\, spanning tender lyricism and spa
 rkling buffo comedy alike.\nA cornerstone of the operatic repertoire — a
 nd a thrilling challenge for any ensemble to perform.\nAugust 14th-16th\, 
 7:30 PM\, Aug 15th\, 2:00pm\nSPSS · 525 Bloor St E\, Toronto\nTickets $10
 –$40
LOCATION:Church of St. Peter and St. Simon
URL:https://www.blogto.com/events/emerging-artist-spotlight-toronto/
X-SOURCE:blogTO
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Grange Festival 2026 - Free family-friendly street fair
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260807T113000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260807T150000
UID:blogto-237050@blogto.com
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://blogto-production2-baselayer-display.blo
 gto.com/events/2026/06/25/original_image81287fbf-2ddf-4255-9cfa-45a0a1dd9f
 9d.jpg
DESCRIPTION:Join us to celebrate Toronto's oldest and most beloved free ou
 tdoor street fair!\nUniversity Settlement will welcome thousand of communi
 ty members to Grange Park on August 7 for the 85th annual Grange Festival\
 , a long-standing Toronto tradition celebrating culture\, connection\, and
  community. The Grange Festival welcomes more than 4500 participants each 
 year and features performances\, community organizations\, activities\, an
 d opportunities for neighbours of all ages to connect. All free thanks to 
 the generosity of the sponsors International Data Financial Services (IFDS
 )\, the Kiwanis Club of Casa Loma and ecobee by Generac. Rain or Shine!\nT
 his multicultural\, intergenerational event will be honoured by the presen
 ce of Mayor Olivia Chow\, Deputy Mayor Ausma Malik and MPP Chris Glover\, 
 highlighting its significance as a cultural landmark in the city.\nAt this
  year’s festival\, attendees will have the opportunity to:\n•	Explore 
 community tables from more than 25 Toronto-based organizations\n•	Enjoy 
 live performances by local artists\, including dance and music representin
 g the city’s diverse cultural communities\, as well as performances from
  University Settlement’s Music & Arts School and Seniors Programs\n•	P
 articipate in free games and activities\, and enjoy free treats\, includin
 g hot dogs\, ice cream\, snow cones\, popcorn\, and cotton candy.\nThe Gra
 nge Festival’s rich history dates to its origins as the Spring Festival 
 in the early 1900s\, when it featured music\, drama\, and neighbourhood pa
 rades. The festival in its current form has been celebrated for 85 years. 
 and stands as a testament to the resilience and unity of Toronto’s diver
 se communities\, with values that closely align with University Settlement
 ’s mission.\nUniversity Settlement is proud to host an event that reflec
 ts its longstanding commitment to supporting families\, seniors\, newcomer
 s\, and immigrants\, and to fostering inclusion\, connection\, and communi
 ty well-being.
URL:https://www.blogto.com/events/grange-festival-2026-free-family-friendl
 y-street-fair-toronto/
X-SOURCE:blogTO
END:VEVENT
